{
 "id": "M02",
 "kind": "echarts",
 "title": "Few budget requests audit exactly the share asked for",
 "section": "appendix",
 "order": 135,
 "caption": "Every cell of the study's budget table: a requested budget on the left, where that monitor's threshold actually landed on the right. A band is drawn only when the served rate misses the request. Thickness is the number of cells.",
 "notes": [
  "24 of 329 cells serve the rate they were asked for. The rest snap to whatever cutoff the monitor's own honest scores allow, which is almost always cheaper than the request.",
  "9 cells serve nothing at all: no honest score sits above the cutoff, so the budget cannot be spent and the monitor catches nothing at that setting.",
  "Not one of the 47 runs can serve a 0.5% request exactly. That column is arithmetic, not monitor quality: 0.5% of 500 honest programs is under three rows.",
  "Data quality: this counts cells, so a monitor with many runs weighs more than one with few. It says how often a requested budget is honoured across the table, not how often a given monitor honours one.",
  "Ported from the data explorer's b1-requested-vs-served view, reading achieved-fpr.csv."
 ],
